# The hooks projects bind through a shim.
#
# AN ENTRY HERE REQUIRES A PUBLISHER THAT OWNS ITS IMPLEMENTATION.
#
# This file declares what projects BIND; it has never declared what anything
# PUBLISHES. Until 2026-08-09 the two sets were maintained separately —
# ARTIFACTS held the publish side, and this header explained at length why the
# lists must not be merged. ARTIFACTS is gone with install-project-hooks.sh, and
# the retirement turns that separation into a condition somebody has to keep:
#
#   a hook may be listed here only when some SURVIVING publisher puts its
#   implementation at ~/.agenticapps/bin/<hook>.sh.
#
# Today that means install-shared-artifact.sh, which publishes exactly four —
# openspec-change-gate.sh, run-plan-review.sh, reviewer-cli.sh, init-project.sh.
# A name outside that set has no publisher at all: adding it would have
# check-shims.sh demand a shim in every fleet repository, and every shim it
# demanded would fail open at run time against a path nothing writes. The
# checker would report a green fleet of hooks that resolve nothing.
#
# So `openspec-change-gate` is the one name a reverse pass could still resolve,
# and it is listed nowhere below for an unrelated reason — the machine-level git
# hook replaced its project binding. Restoring a publisher is the other way to
# satisfy the condition, and it is a change, not an edit to this file.
#
# One hook name per line, without the .sh. Blank lines and # comments ignored.
#
# THE SET IS EMPTY, AND THAT IS THE END STATE RATHER THAN AN ACCIDENT.
# Both entries left on 2026-08-08, for different reasons:
#
#   openspec-change-gate — its project binding is replaced by the machine-level
#     git hook. core.hooksPath is bound globally, so one published pre-commit
#     gates every enrolled repository and no project needs a shim to reach it.
#
#   database-sentinel — removed WITH the host-specific surface it lived on, not
#     migrated off it. It was a Claude PreToolUse hook, so it only ever
#     protected one of the provisioned hosts; centralising it machine-level
#     would keep that same single-host blindness while making it look fleet-wide.
#     What it intercepted is recorded as LOST in the projects-bind-not-copy
#     change. It is not reassigned, and nothing here should say it was.
#
# An empty declaration is not an absent one. check-shims.sh distinguishes them:
# absent is a broken instrument and exits non-zero; empty reports that nothing
# was checked and refuses to print the conformance sentence.
